A||||30||fiero56||auto Fiero questions on driveability, etc.||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||04-11-2001||06:38 PM||||Wondering how auto Fieros do. What scares me is the 125 is only a three speed. Is first geared well enough to allow you to jump into traffic and get moving quick, or do they dog up to speed? How's highway driving? What RPM are you turning? <BR>How is the 4 cyl auto. Is it really slow, or will it get moving when needed? How's the 6 cyl feel? Any particular complaints about them, other than being auto?||||reg||5
Z||000001||william_thorniii||04-11-2001||07:03 PM||||Drew,<P>You are more than welcome to borrow my 87 for as long as you like... assuming you lend me one of your vehicles to cruise around in.<P>To answer your questions on the hwy I turn near 3000 times per minute at 70 MPH and 2400 or so at 60 MPH.  I will have to get back with you on the gas mileage.  Typically I get near 32 MPH out of it with my mostly highway driving style.  I just replaced the plugs, wires, O2 sensor and a few other goodies that could significantly affect that number.  I have yet to get a full tank of typical driving in with the new goodies... so I will be able to verify this in a few weeks.  I can tell you that during one trip I averaged nearly 38 MPG.  I have yet to repeat that feat... must have had a good tail wind.  <P>I find the derivability to be nice for a daily driver.  I also do not find myself in the wrong gear coming out of a corner, which is common in autos with more gears to choose from.  The damn thing is rock solid even after running it quite low on fluid during that hose mishap.  All in all, my auto DIS Duke has provided me with nearly 50,000 virtually trouble free miles.  <P>It all comes down to personal preference and driving style.  I do not think that you would miss much with an auto.  They are quite nice for driving in heavy traffic.  <P>That said, I am considering getting another coupe with the 5-spd.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Galen||04-11-2001||07:09 PM||||You know that makes my auto sound bad. I think I needa replace the filter, I turn WAY too high a rpm at 70.. up around 4grand. :/ that sucks majorly hehe.. <P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>Spinrpg.com - Free URL & e-mail Forwarding</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||04-11-2001||08:47 PM||||I don't mind my 88coupe auto much. The 3 speed is kind of old technology though. Its not that fast starting out with, I hit 0-60 in about 9 seconds. The biggest problem I have with mins is the TC. At around 35-40 when the TC kicks in on 3rd gear my engine is just very slightly chugging, which bothers me    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. I thought I remember the 84s didn't have a TC though, which is bad for highway driving(higher rpm). Heres what the shift points seem to be on my auto.....<BR>1-37mph_1st gear<BR>38-70mph_2nd gear<BR>71-124_3rd gear<P>Fastest I had my coupe was 124mph (I couldn't go any faster), that was just in the yellow bar rpm range    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I don't normally get up to those speeds, just on quiet Canadian highways.<P>Also, I've heard that fieros had 2 different auto Trans gear ratios. Does anyone know about this?<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||04-11-2001||09:37 PM||||I've only driven an auto Fiero once or twice, and since it wasn't mine I didn't <B>drive</B> it or get much of a feel for it. Rode in an auto v-6 once, but it was only for about the length of a city block. <P>There is a lot of highway construction going on right now, so I've been taking the surface roads, and the stop and go commute is driving me crazy, especially inching forward all the time with a manual. I figure in the next month or two, I will know if an auto is in my future. If so, will either sell the SE, or look for some kind of trade. Don't even know if will go for 6 or 4, don't have any real experience with the 4, and none with auto 6. <BR>Plus, it is nice sometimes, just to hop in and put a car in drive.<P>Still looking for other views about the auto.  I spoke with TerryK briefly about a 4-speed auto, but he mentioned regretting putting one in his, and would rather go back to 125, but not worth the effort at this time.||||reg||1
Z||000005||btoth||04-11-2001||10:18 PM||||I really like my auto GT.  It shift right before the red line when flooring it in 1st and 2nd.  It does run at a high RPM at highway speeds compared to other cars I've driven, but I'm sure that those have had more HP than my 2.8 puts out.  The advantage (?) to the higher RPM is that when I let off the gas it can slow itself down which I like (seeing as how my breaks suck).  And I do enjoy downshifting into 2nd for red lights... slows the car down quick (and helps with the tail gaters!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>Of course, I've never driven a manual Fiero.||||reg||7
Z||000006||tgowens||04-11-2001||10:36 PM||||Drew, you may remember my gold '87 GT from the Louisville show - it is an automatic. I have a blue '87 GT which will be just a show car and it too is an automatic. I am restoring an '87 GT for my daughter which has the Getrag 5-speed (because that's what she wanted!) -- my preference (because of both road driving and stop and go is the automatic -- like William said tho - it depends on your style of driving. I <I>feel</I> that the stick is quicker than the auto but at my age all I want to do is drive - got tired of shifting in my drag racing days..........now it's just put it in D and go.........but - it really is personal preference. When I bring mine back there you are more than welcome to DRIVE it (I have tools and can fix what ya break!!)||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||04-11-2001||10:44 PM||||Terry, I'm not looking for a stop-light racer. I've got my Formula for that    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. But, if I get an auto, I want to make sure it can keep up with traffic, particularly when traffic is heavy, and the only openings require getting up to speed quickly, traffic here in c'ville has increased dramatically over the last few years.<P>Terry, is engine noise very loud with the engine spinning as fast as it does with an auto on the highway. My 4-speed v-6 is quite noisy on the highway at 2,800rpm at 70.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||DavidHenshaw||04-11-2001||11:11 PM||||When I was shopping for my first Fiero last summer I wanted a manual V6, but it seemed like all the V6's I looked at were automatic. I ended up buying an 86 GT with automatic and was a little concerned when I found out it was a three speed. Since then I've put about 10,000 miles on the car in all types of driving and I have no complaints with the tranny. <P>From a standing start it will rip a$$. Oops, sorry ladies. In fact unless you're hauling (see above) it will stay in 3rd all the time because the V6 has lots of torque. The TC mentioned in another post which comes on at about 40mph is a little annoying but I'm used to it now.<P>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||04-11-2001||11:56 PM||||well Drew, what can I tell ya -- I'm out here driving in California freeway traffic all the time (where they do 85 in the fast lane usually) and I have nooooooo problem getting up to speed quickly off of a ramp or to change lanes! (and I don't even have to give it all its got!) -- I think I'm somewhere around 3k at 70-72mph.........believe me, I think I know your concerns and I think you would be happy with an auto - like I said, you can drive mine and see (it may be awhile before I have one of them back there though - plans are to take the blue one to Ed Park's Swap Meet since it will have the '88 style rear suspension on it -- I figure a 2,000+ road test will check it out pretty good!) - but, the little 2.8 automatic is a fine driving car, especially in traffic!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Uaana||04-12-2001||12:15 AM||||Hate to be the voice of dissention here but..<BR>If the auto has one failing it would be hard corners. I've had 2 now and for regular driving, freeways, stop and go they are just fine. but start throwning them into some heavy corners and they start searching for a gear to be in. I remember once when I and a friend were playing through some low speed -30mph but tight and it kept feeling like it had popped out of gear (high revs) <BR>I belive this has something do do with no baffles for the fluid.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||04-12-2001||12:26 AM||||My 86 SE V-6 has the stock auto. I drove it for years without any problems. You will have no problems pulling into traffic. 2 years ago I installed a shift kit. This make the trans shift slightly harder under WOT, and prevents the torque converter bleed down when cornering like a madman. Even in stock form with the stock tire sizes I was able to get a "chirp" out of the tires at a light. <BR>An auto Fiero is slower than a stick but still fairly quick.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||tgowens||04-12-2001||01:28 AM||||well Drew, there ya have it - the end result here is, drive an auto in traffic and when you have to go around a hard corner drive back home and get the Formula!! -- like I said, personal preference - personally, I think everyone should have one of each!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||kslish||04-12-2001||08:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>I spoke with TerryK briefly about a 4-speed auto, but he mentioned regretting putting one in his, and would rather go back to 125, but not worth the effort at this time.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What did terryk not like about the 4 speed auto?  I was thinking about this swap.<P>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||1FST2M6||04-12-2001||08:25 AM||||i LOVE you automatic! she'l chirp into second, and she'll power brake just as well as a camaro or mustang...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> it runs consistant and quiet. pulls 9.8@67mph in the 1/8th and will do it over and over and over... <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fst9.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fst9.jpg</A>  <P>i've nver driven an Auto 4cyl... but RodrV6s 84 4spd is a pretty strong car.. i'm sure the 4-auto will keep up with traffic... ||||reg||1
Z||000015||fiero56||04-12-2001||08:38 AM||||Lots of praise for the auto so far. When I was watching for "the pefect deal" two years ago, I was looking for a manual, but was willing to go for auto if the price was right. That's when the Formula fell in my lap. Then, last fall, I went to check out a non-running '86SE 2.8 4-speed to post about here in case somebody was interested in it. I ended up buying that one on the spot too. There don't seem to be many automatics around here. I still love my manual Fieros, and would never be without at least one stick, but an auto would be nice once in a while.<BR>I guess I'll have to try both 6 and 4 before I decide.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 04-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||ChadMan||04-12-2001||08:42 AM||||Drew, I drove my father-in-law's 86 SE V6 automatic around town a lot, then across the country.  It was great as far as automatics go -- smooth, quiet, a great boulevard cruiser/ grand touring car and a better commuter car than any of my stick-shift Fieros.  It was slower than my 4-sp V6 SE, but not a whole lot slower.<P>But I thought it was definitely missing something from a "fun" standpoint.<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 04-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fformula88||04-12-2001||08:45 AM||||My experience with the Fiero auto has been nothing but great.  My fathers previous car was a 4 cyl auto Fiero.  The car was no rocket, but it could get going too.  Don't expect to race and Rustangs or anything and you will be fine.  Milage was about 26 MPG city.  Plus, the trans was still shifting great after 130K miles, and the only service done to it was one fluid change about 60K.  <P>My Formula also has the auto, and has been equally reliable.  It now has 80K on it, but still delivers very good shifts without any slipping.  The computer is also very good at deciding when to shift.  It keeps the car in its powerband better than most transmissions I have driven.<P>As for the 4-spd... thats whats in my dads 85 V6 SE.  That car flies!  The difference is from a stop... there is just more power available from a stop than the auto can deliver.  Once moving, I don't feel a very big difference betweem them.  Oh, and its a lot more fun too!  (not that the auto isn't fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierosig2.JPG"> <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000018||tgowens||04-12-2001||11:13 AM||||Drew, go with the 6, you'll be glad you did........||||reg||1
Z||000019||terryk||04-12-2001||11:37 AM||||Maybe you misunderstood me or I mis-typed. I don't regret putting one in. I only have 4spd autos with my 3800SC's. Nothing to regret.<P>I wouldn't put a 4spd auto on a 2.8L/3.1L V6 though. Nothing to gain. I liked my 125C.||||reg||5
Z||000020||fierojunky||04-12-2001||12:21 PM||||Okay - I'm lost - mabey just stupid - I didn't know there was a 3 speed auto - the only 3 speed i thought there was - was in the Indy 3 speed manual.  I thought the autos were 4 speeds.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000021||fiero56||04-12-2001||12:23 PM||||There was no 4-speed auto, and no three speed manual.||||reg||1
Z||000022||drednot||04-12-2001||12:35 PM||||my 1985 Manual 5 speed 4cyl turns over exactly 3000 rpm at 85 mph.  with that trans, my car is sort of slow from start up, but once it gets over 100 mph (est) it relly flys!  I only wish I had the 120 mph spedometer so I can tell how fast I am going! ||||reg||10
Z||000023||bHooper||04-12-2001||12:54 PM||||i have driven exactly <B>ONE</B> automatic Fiero in my life... Bob Englert's Blue '87 GT.  That car has a shift kit, and we aren't certain, but it may have a 3.1 liter.  Either way, driving his car the two or three times that I have, has made me fall in love with the auto.<P>my second Fiero is an automatic.  i haven't driven the car yet, or seen it for that matter, but i know first hand it is a good ridding car (i'm getting it from Doug Kinney).  <P>mind you, i will be keeping my 5spd Gt for fun.  i just wanted soemthing for a nice daily driver.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000024||ChadMan||04-12-2001||01:58 PM||||Hey, fierojunky, don't feel bad...I thought the Fiero had a 4-sp auto too until I realized what I thought was the 3-4 shift was actually the torque convertor lockup.||||reg||1
Z||000025||fierojunky||04-12-2001||04:08 PM||||I feel really stupid - i have an auto and could have sworn it has four gears - but to answer the original question - the guy how owned it before me put a shift kit in and i have absolutely no problems getting on the freeways  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000026||william_thorniii||04-12-2001||04:11 PM||||Drew,<P>I would like to rescind my earlier statement.  At 70 MPH my engine is more accurately spinning 2850 revolutions.  I have little to no problems getting in and out of trafic in town or otherwise.  <P>William||||reg||1
Z||000027||fiero56||04-13-2001||12:02 PM||||About the 4-speed auto: it would be nice to have a nice highway cruising gear. What do the typical GM 4-speed autos run the engine at 70mph?||||reg||1
Z||000028||wkayl||04-13-2001||08:51 PM||||Here is an 86 GT with an automatic.  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/instone_1.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/twocars.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||grinthock||04-13-2001||08:55 PM||||I have a 87 GT AUTO, changed the TC put in a TC with a higher stall, it was pulled from a 3800SC Conversion job (he was going to keep his standard) and it runs GREAT, WAY better shifting, more power, before the TC the car dogged it between gears large, although if you are going to do this, ALSO get a tranny cooler!   Check out my car<BR> <A HREF="http://grinthock.2y.net/87GT.html" TARGET=_blank>http://grinthock.2y.net/87GT.html</A> ||||reg||12
Z||000030||kslish||04-14-2001||09:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>About the 4-speed auto: it would be nice to have a nice highway cruising gear. What do the typical GM 4-speed autos run the engine at 70mph?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My front-drive Cutlass Supreme with the 4-speed auto turns about 1800 rpms at 70 mph I think...been a while since I drove it on the highway (too busy driving my Fieros).<P>The main reason I'm also looking into this swap is for highway cruising and fuel economy.  My Cutlass can get about 30-31 mpg crusing all day at 70 mph. <P>Ken S.<BR>'87 GT and '86 SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> <P>||||reg||1
